# /etc/portage/env per-package fallback ladder

Default policy:
    Packages first build with the most aggressive global tier in make.conf:
    clang/lld, libc++ by default, Polly, OpenMP, ThinLTO, unified LTO,
    whole-program vtables, -O3, -ffast-math, and the forced runtime link
    libraries in LDFLAGS only.

    If a package fails, demote one risky axis at a time so the cause remains
    diagnosable. Do not globally remove fast-math, Polly, LTO, OpenMP,
    libc++, -Bsymbolic, or bleeding-edge language targets merely because
    they are risky. They are the experiment.

Demotion rule:
    Prefer the narrowest fallback that explains the failure.
    Do not demote to plain.conf unless less conservative tiers have failed
    or the package is known to be hostile to the aggressive toolchain.

Promotion rule:
    After LLVM/Clang, libc++, Python, Ruby, ROCm, or major package updates,
    retry a few demoted packages. Remove stale package.env demotions when the
    current toolchain can build the package at a more aggressive tier again.

Fallback ladder, least to most conservative:
    default
        make.conf aggressive tier: Polly + OpenMP + ThinLTO + unified LTO +
        vtable optimization + libc++ runtime selection + link-only forced
        runtime libraries.

    no-polly.conf
        Removes Polly and branch-target alignment first. Try this when an
        assembler, compiler, or build system rejects -mllvm/-polly flags.

    no-polly-no-whole-program-vtables.conf
        Removes Polly and only whole-program vtables while keeping O3,
        OpenMP, ThinLTO, unified LTO, libc++, and vtable strictness. Use
        for LLVM/Clang self-build links where whole-program vtables expose
        a hidden or unexported LLVM destructor. Apply to llvm-core/llvm as
        the libLLVM.so provider before retrying llvm-core/clang.

    no-unified-lto.conf
        Keeps Polly, O3, OpenMP, strict vtable pointers, libc++, and plain
        ThinLTO while removing only unified-LTO extras. Use for mixed C/Rust
        or other links where lld reports incompatible unified-LTO bitcode.

    thin-lto-only.conf
        Keeps Polly and ThinLTO but removes unified-LTO and vtable extras.
        Try this for hidden construction vtable or whole-program-vtable link
        failures.

    O3.conf
        Uses an O3 tier without Polly/OpenMP/-fno-plt while keeping full LTO.

    O3-no-vtables.conf
        O3.conf without the vtable/whole-program extras, keeping only plain
        unified LTO where needed.

    no-openmp.conf
        Removes OpenMP from compile/link flags while keeping the rest of the
        aggressive stack close to default.

    no-lto.conf
        Turns off LTO, including Rust LTO. Use for packages whose link step
        fails specifically because of LTO.

    no-lto-no-link-libs.conf
        Turns off host LTO/unified-LTO decoration while also avoiding the
        forced -l* runtime library set. Use for device/runtime links that
        must emit LLVM bitcode themselves, such as the AMDGCN OpenMP device
        runtime.

    no-polly-no-lto.conf
        LLVM/Clang self-build fallback. Drops Polly and all compile/link LTO
        while keeping O3, fast-math, OpenMP, libc++, strict vtable pointers,
        and the non-LTO aggressive linker tier. Use when clang/libclang's
        ThinLTO link keeps references to hidden LLVM inline/destructor
        symbols that libLLVM.so does not export. Apply to llvm-core/llvm
        too before retrying llvm-core/clang, because clang's early tools
        link against LLVM static archives such as libLLVMSupport.a and
        libLLVMTableGen.a.

    no-link-libs.conf
        Keeps the aggressive compile/LTO tier but avoids forcing the extra
        runtime libraries on the link line.

    O2.conf
        Plain O2, native CPU, and libc++ selection. This is a broad fallback.

    plain.conf
        Plain O2, native CPU, and ccache disabled. Treat as the last resort.

C++ runtime escape hatches:
    clang-libstdcxx.conf
        Build with clang/lld while selecting libstdc++ for C++ compilation
        and linking. Prefer this when libc++ exposes C++ library or ABI
        compatibility problems, such as Gamescope atomic shared_ptr issues.

    libstdcxx-headers.conf
        Clang with libstdc++ runtime selection through the active GCC
        install. Use only for packages that hard-require libstdc++
        internals.

ROCm econf policy:
    rocm-extra-econf.conf
        Opt-in --with-rocm=/usr/lib64 for autotools packages that actually
        consume it. Do not set this globally; unrelated configure scripts may
        reject unknown options. Apply through package.env only for selected
        packages such as sys-apps/hwloc.

ccache and sandbox policy:
    no-ccache-only.conf
        Disable ccache only. Use this when ccache breaks a build but sandbox
        is not involved.

    unsafe-no-ccache-no-sandbox.conf
        Disable ccache plus sandbox/usersandbox/network-sandbox. This is
        intentionally invasive. Use only when sandboxing itself is known to
        break the build, and document why in package.env.

Local-only hooks:
    zfs-cachyos-rt-local-license-hack.conf
        Points sys-fs/zfs at the local-only bashrc hook below.

    sys-fs/zfs-cachyos-rt-local-license-hack
        LOCAL-ONLY CachyOS RT/preempt ZFS license metadata hack. This is not
        a clean upstream ZFS fix and must not be treated as redistributable
        packaging guidance.

Other package-specific profiles:
    gcc.conf
        Build with GCC-valid flags instead of clang flags.

    glibc.conf
        Default-ish flags without the global warning downgrade set.

    rust-llvm21.conf
        dev-lang/rust pin to clang/LLVM 21 tooling.

    rust-no-lto.conf
        Conservative RUSTFLAGS/CFLAGS for broken cargo packages.

    highway-noavx512.conf
        O3/native with AVX512 disabled for dev-cpp/highway consumers.

    include-string.conf
        O3 plus CXXFLAGS --include string for packages missing an include.

    mount-defines.conf
        Extra include/define handling for xfsprogs mount API fallout.

    gimp.conf
        Package-specific GIMP fallback.

Diagnostic quick map:
    If assembler rejects -mllvm:
        try no-polly.conf first.

    If C++ libc++ symbols, headers, or ABI assumptions fail:
        try clang-libstdcxx.conf or libstdcxx-headers.conf.

    If LTO link fails:
        if the package is LLVM/Clang itself, try
        no-polly-no-whole-program-vtables.conf first on llvm-core/llvm and
        llvm-core/clang. If the same hidden LLVM destructor remains in
        libclang's ThinLTO link, apply no-polly-no-lto.conf to both
        llvm-core/llvm and llvm-core/clang before broadening further.
        try thin-lto-only.conf, then no-lto.conf.

    If OpenMP runtime/linking causes configure or link failures:
        try no-openmp.conf.

    If configure rejects global econf arguments:
        remove the global econf use and apply rocm-extra-econf.conf only to
        packages that need it.

    If ccache breaks build but sandbox does not:
        use no-ccache-only.conf.

    If sandbox itself breaks build:
        use unsafe-no-ccache-no-sandbox.conf and document why.

Hook files:
    Files in env/<category>/<package-or-local-hook-name> are bashrc-style
    hooks with functions such as post_src_prepare. They are separate from
    package.env variable profiles. If a package.env entry needs one of these
    hooks, point PORTAGE_BASHRC at the hook from a small .conf file.
